  "image_name": "03a63ca78024d06e8c8acc962ab706f8.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a malaria drug-discovery pipeline organized around the parasite life cycle. At the center, a mosquito links three major stages—transmission, liver stage, and blood stage—shown as a circular progression from mosquito to liver to red blood cells and back.\n\nAround this core, the outer ring shows how compounds can be screened or validated at each stage using different Plasmodium species and model systems. High-throughput screening is illustrated for blood-stage *P. falciparum*, where infected erythrocytes are exposed to a compound library and parasite proliferation is measured. Another screen targets *P. falciparum* gametocytes to assess parasite death, reflecting transmission-blocking activity. Liver-stage screening uses *P. cynomolgi* sporozoites infecting monkey hepatocytes, with imaging of small forms.\n\nThe figure also highlights in vivo validation steps. Blood-stage efficacy can be tested in mice infected with *P. berghei* or *P. yoelii* by monitoring parasite clearance. Transmission-blocking validation appears to involve treating infected hosts, allowing mosquito feeding, and then counting oocysts. Liver-stage and relapse models use sporozoite infection in rodents or monkeys, followed by test compounds to evaluate development of blood-stage infection or relapse episodes.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes stage-specific antimalarial discovery across screening and animal validation platforms.",
